WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.839 --> 00:00:02.950 align:start position:0%
 
every day  it arrives  produced by the truckload donated to  this food bank in northwest london  and today the frontline in the battle  against poverty is also the frontline of  a cost of living crisis  this is our food hub  with inflation now at a 30-year high the  pressures on households are becoming  ever clearer  our queues are increasing just last week  we served 180 people outside our queue  we've seen that increase by 40 percent  have you got anything for me like meats  or  dinners  karen is among hundreds of people  reliant on the support of this place why  it comes back to the cost of living  food is going up in price  um gas and electricity the council tax  the rent i find it really hard to sleep  get nightmares  because because of those financial  pressures yeah we've gone without eating  we just had toast for dinner  yeah  sorry i'm getting a bit emotional  but only when you appear beneath the  surface of the inflation statistics do  you see just how widespread these price  rises are  what is inflation it's ultimately about  the rate at which the typical shopping  basket of goods and services is rising  in the past year and the striking thing  is just how fast they're rising bread up  by about four and a half percent  margarine up by 27  margarine uh apples up by about a fifth  beef and veal up by seven percent you  get the idea that's just food well it's  worth the same avocados have fallen in  price but take it all together and  you're talking about remarkable  increases in the cost of living  and that's before you consider things  like the wholesale gas price  its trajectory now even higher than the  last time the bank of england looked at  it says the governor started to go back  in the market pricing towards  where it was pre-coded  now it doesn't come off until next  middle of next year towards the tail end  of next year  so that is a big shift  that of course raises the prospect of  another hike in interest rates perhaps  as soon as next month  at this factory in the west midlands  where they turn steel into covers and  gratings it is metal prices which are  the big inflationary force  in a space of just under 18 months we've  seen the price of steel increased by  over 100  which obviously had significant  implications on our manufacturing costs  to our customers  these are just snapshots from across the  country which is seeing the resurgence  of something economists thought they  vanquished long ago  there will be more inflation to come  ed conway sky news

